title the switchboard that crossed a kernel -- FORA31 lands, and the routing stops being ours
prompt Open as SILENCE under the INNER LOOP Standing block -- roster cold then hot, reds first as standfast, QA on touch, council rota row N mod 5, prose witnesses on prose sends, stamp-and-name, ASCII-first, READ SCOPE -- then pick up the running thread and take the infrastructure lane.
think Reds-first read the pin honestly and came back empty for my lane. %291, %295, %301's three rooms and %306 are all Keaton's word or a seat. %311's two ratchets read as Sound's by the row's own sentence, and I checked rather than took the sentence: glow/.cache/gate_barket_sample_argv.rye lowers to a program whose whole body is `return if (barket_gate(sample) == expect) 0 else 1;` and prints nothing at all. The row is right -- the plant must speak before a witness can read it, and making it speak is the language, which is my star's custody.
think That left the granted door. Three documents disagreed about whether FORA31 was gated, so I read rather than picked: constel/README.md says the socket "waits for the maintainer's word", the charter's plan section says "on Keaton's word, and not before", and the charter's own Status line and the card both read GRANTED 20260827 (approve all doors), with the card naming FORA31 twice as Kyri's next door. The Status line supersedes the plan section it heads, and the card is the living pin. Granted.
think The custody read was the one worth being slow about. constel/switchboard.rye's own head calls the real socket "a later, GATED round" -- yet what it names as gated is gate %2 (real hardware or any wire) and gate %4 (real Kumara), and this rung touches neither: 127.0.0.1 only, ports derived from a seat index, consonant-only names that cannot parse as a live @p, no key and no fund. The precedent is already standing in-tree -- comlink/rehearsal_wire.rye crosses three real processes over localhost UDP and has sat on the cadence roster since 20260828.011621. A real transport between provably fake piers is a thing this bench already does under a guard.
think Before building on the pen I proved the pen runs here rather than assuming it. Built comlink/rehearsal_wire.rye on this Darwin arm64 bench and ran the fleet: three processes, three ports, "GREEN -- three processes, one pen, every voice verified over localhost". That reading also chose the API. rehearsal_wire reaches std.Io.net -- IpAddress.bind, sock.send, sock.receive -- rather than a hand-rolled sockaddr, which sidesteps REDS %282's BSD-versus-Linux layout trap entirely on the one host where it bites. The dialect family is a thin-edge fault and the thin edge includes the host.
think constel/socket.rye mirrors constel/switchboard.rye method for method -- size, stand_up, member, deliver, receive, sky, handshake_through -- with the [max_piers]channel.Channel array replaced by [max_piers]net.Socket bound at base_port + slot on 127.0.0.1. base_port is 38512, past 38500, which is rehearsal_wire's third ship and the highest port any seated module binds. receive takes a bounded receiveTimeout and answers Silent rather than blocking; the module names three waits (receive_wait_ms 250, silence_wait_ms 120, hold_ms 3000) so nothing it offers can hang.
think The crux leg is handshake_through: the same FORA4 greeting, ack and session, now four real datagrams, with the sealed Session read back off a socket rather than out of an array. It completed first try -- xnb and xnc agreed across the kernel.
obs The leg I nearly shipped vacuous was `silent`. It answers by calling receiveTimeout and reading a Timeout as silence, so a receiveTimeout that failed for any unrelated reason would make silent return true forever and the bystander proof would be a needle the harness supplied -- REDS %311's exact shape, one module over. So the selftest now spends a whole crossing proving silent reads LOUD on a loaded descriptor before it is trusted to read SILENT on a drained one. It costs one datagram and it is the difference between a proof and a sentence.
obs `silent` consumes what it finds, because a datagram socket offers no non-destructive peek through this API. That is written into its own doc comment rather than left to be discovered by whoever calls it next -- the honest version of a wart is a named one.
obs The witness needed a refusal it could read, not merely count. The answer was to make the descriptors prove they are real kernel resources: `hold` binds all eight seats and releases on its own after 3000 ms, and leg B races a selftest against it. The racing board exits 1 and says `error: BindRefused` with the kernel's own ADDRINUSE in the trace beneath it. A simulation cannot fail that way. The hold is bounded on purpose so a red here can never strand this bench's ports.
obs A wrong name of my own, caught by that leg. The bind failure was returning error.SendFailed, because I had one error for "the kernel said no". A bind and a send fail for different reasons and a reader chasing one should not be handed the other's word, so BindRefused is named beside it -- and the witness now asserts on that name, which is what made the mistake visible at all.
obs I proved the witness can red rather than only that it passes. Removed the in-flight byte flip from the tamper leg, rebuilt, ran: the selftest returned TamperSlipped and the witness failed at line 44. Restored, rebuilt, GREEN again. A guard proven only in the passing direction cannot be told from a bypass, and this one was proven from both sides on real bytes.
obs The MODULES roster guard was measured red BEFORE the row was written, which is the honest order -- unrostered=1 on constel/socket.rye, then unrostered=0 and phantom=0 after, with the guard's own planted cases still biting.
obs QA on touch, four artifacts, one frame opened and closed by measurement. constel/MODULES.md A+ at 98 (meter). constel/README.md B at 83 (door) -- stands. constel/LADDER.md reads F at 56 (field), so I measured whether I caused it: HEAD's copy reads F at 59, register 34 and reach 0, so the file has read F under its own declared setting since 20260824.104946. At meter it reads A+ at 98, and the reason is the class -- 32 rung entries are witness headers in prose, where refusal is the subject and negatives are uncapped by design. I did NOT flip the declared setting: the file is genuinely mixed, its head and road-on narrative are Field prose that should keep a ceiling, and reversing another lap's seated declaration on a mixed document is a design word rather than an on-touch sweep. Booked instead, with both numbers. I did rewrite my own rung into shorter sentences; it moved the composite not at all (56 before and after), which is itself the measurement that the frame is the file's rather than my edit's.
obs Not mine, and named to the peer rather than raced: their tools/equinox fold landed at 17:13 while I was in constel/, taking tools/gen/chapter from 434 to 173 -- exactly the 173 the 163333 survey predicted for shape B. 258 files moved, and 201 of those basenames are still cited at their old tools/gen/chapter path somewhere in the tree. The cold roster caught one as a hard red, foundations_link, links_read=500 links_broken=1, and tool_path_resolve answers recovered-by-index for it. I left every one of them alone and sent the measurement.
obs The dead-letter box was worth opening. stash@{0}, stashed by my own round-open at 17:11:21, holds a 310-file 852-line repoint of tools/gen/chapter -> tools/equinox/witness with no file moves in it -- an earlier attempt at the half the peer has now done properly. Committed alone it would have pointed 852 references at a room that did not exist. Told the peer where it is so they do not later mistake it for lost work.
obs The rota computed row 3 -- N is git rev-list --count HEAD at 3638, and 3638 mod 5 is 3. My previous Silence lap read row 4, so this is a backward jump rather than a repeat, caused by the peer's commits landing between laps; no hand advance was owed. Water, Venus, care and flow -- and it read the lap. The thin edge is exactly what this rung is: the 31 rungs below it stay in the happy zone, pure and fast, while this one spends real ports and real processes and is budgeted at cadence rather than lap for that reason. The row also says the thin edge includes the host itself, which is the BSD dialect family, which is the trap std.Io.net let me walk past.
obs The card is full, and I met it by arithmetic rather than by reading it. construction/ITINERARY.md stands at 24,570 bytes at HEAD against the 24,576 it declares -- six bytes of headroom. The round-close row the card's own Prior-laps rule mandates could not be written: my first row put it at 24,616, and the booked LADDER frame beside it at 24,805. I shaved the row three times to land at 24,573 and dropped the booked paragraph to the log entirely. Said plainly, this is %318's class from the other side -- that row was two pins crossing a bound and shipping; this is a pin with no room left for the line it requires, where the overflow depends on how long a lap chose its own log sprig. Not booked to REDS this lap: the pin and the ledger are both the peer's open surfaces right now and I said I would not touch either until they answer. Surfaced instead.
obs The full hot pass did not run, and the reason is worth recording because this is the fourth consecutive Silence lap to record it. The cold roster opened at 17:11 and was still inside its cadence choirs 45 minutes later -- caravan_suite_witness and the equinox choir -- so a second full pass beside it would have had two rosters building Rye modules into the same bin/ rooms, which is REDS %281's shadow race exactly. What I ran instead is a real hot pass through the runner's own machinery, scoped by its single-guard argument: `--hot fora_socket` and `--hot constel_module_roster`, both reading staged_uncommitted=9, tree_at_open equal to tree_at_close, tree_moved=no, run_verdict=ok. Four more guards ran directly on metal beside them -- tame_style_check, width-check, witness_reach (unreached 1140 against a ceiling of 1154), tracked_link (verdict=ok). Said plainly: this lap's change is proven, and the whole-tree hot green is not claimed.
obs `deliver` shipped with only its refusal exercised. Every crossing leg used `deliver_from`, so the medium-speaking face was proven to say NoSuchPier and never proven to deliver anything -- a send path nothing runs. Leg 3 now spends its LOUD crossing on `deliver` and its byte-comparison crossing on `deliver_from`, so both faces cross the kernel and the selftest says so in one line.
